  // ── PRIORITY RETENTION CONCEPTS (PRINCE2 7 Foundation) ───────────────────
  retentionGapConcepts: [
    {"label": "Practices Replaced Themes; Five Integrated Elements", "parentTopic": "Integrated Elements & the 7-7-7 Structure", objective: "5.1", "keyword": "PRINCE2 7 calls the seven areas of ongoing management PRACTICES (business case, organizing, quality, plans, risk, issues, progress); In version 7 practices replaced the 6th edition's Themes. The method is built from FIVE integrated elements: principles (7), people, practices (7), processes (7) and project context. So the 7-7-7 hook covers only three of the five. PEOPLE is an element, not an eighth practice: it sits across the others because people apply the principles, practices and processes to fit the context. A quick test: a practice is one of the seven named management areas; people is one level up."},
    {"label": "Seven Performance Aspects and Tolerances", "parentTopic": "Sustainability & Performance Aspects", objective: "5.6", "keyword": "PRINCE2 7 has SEVEN aspects of project performance: benefits, cost, time, quality, scope, sustainability and risk. Tolerances can be set against all seven, at project, stage and work package level. SUSTAINABILITY is the addition in version 7 (the 6th edition had six), and it covers environmental, social and economic objectives, not just the environment. It is also one of the nine management approaches. A forecast breach of any one of the seven is an exception and is escalated to the next management level."},
    {"label": "Manage by Exception: Who Sets Tolerance, When to Escalate", "parentTopic": "Manage by Exception", objective: "4.5", "keyword": "A tolerance is the permitted deviation from a target before the current level must escalate. Tolerance flows DOWN: the business layer (outside the project) sets project tolerances for the project board, the board sets stage tolerances for the project manager, and the project manager sets work package tolerances for the team manager. Escalation flows UP one level at a time, triggered by a FORECAST breach, not just an actual one. The project manager raises an exception report; the board decides what to do. Nobody sets their own tolerance."},
    {"label": "Executive, Senior User and Senior Supplier: Three Interests", "parentTopic": "Organizing Practice", objective: "1.2", "keyword": "The project board represents three interests. BUSINESS: the Executive, a single person who owns the business case and is accountable for the project's success (the Executive is not the project manager). USER: the Senior User represents those who will use the outputs and realise the benefits. SUPPLIER: the Senior Supplier represents those who provide the expertise and resources to build the products and is accountable for their quality. The Executive is always one person; the user and supplier roles can be shared by several. The project manager works day to day within tolerances; the board does not manage day to day."},
    {"label": "Checkpoint vs Highlight vs Exception Report", "parentTopic": "Progress Practice", objective: "1.7", "keyword": "Reports travel one level up. A CHECKPOINT REPORT goes from the team manager to the project manager, comparing actual with planned progress on a work package; its frequency is set in the work package. A HIGHLIGHT REPORT goes from the project manager to the project board at the frequency in the communication management approach, summarising progress, risks and issues; it reports and asks for no decision. An EXCEPTION REPORT goes from the project manager to the board only when a stage or project is forecast to go outside tolerance, and it carries options and a recommendation because the board must decide."},
    {"label": "Risk vs Issue and the Six Issue Types", "parentTopic": "Issues Practice", objective: "1.6", "keyword": "A RISK is an uncertain future event (a threat or an opportunity) and lives in the risk register. An ISSUE is something happening now that needs a response and lives in the issue register. A risk that materialises becomes an issue; an unexpected problem that was never a risk starts as an issue. PRINCE2 7 names six types of issue: problem, concern, external event, business opportunity, request for change and off-specification (some official material counts five, pairing problem with concern, while the Quick Reference Guide counts six, so learn the named list, not the count). A request for change asks to alter something that was agreed; an off-specification reports that something agreed is not being met."},
    {"label": "Baselines, Records and Reports; the Nine Management Approaches", "parentTopic": "Management Products & Management Approaches", objective: "5.5", "keyword": "Management products fall into three categories by changeability. BASELINES are approved and cannot change without formal approval (the project initiation documentation, plans, business case). RECORDS are living logs updated freely (risk register, issue register, quality register, lessons log). REPORTS are snapshots sent at a moment and not revised (highlight, checkpoint, end stage reports). PRINCE2 7 has NINE management approaches, all baselined within the project initiation documentation: benefits, change, commercial, communication, digital and data, issue, quality, risk and sustainability."},
    {"label": "Change Management vs Change Control", "parentTopic": "Leading Successful Change", objective: "3.2", "keyword": "The two terms are different. CHANGE MANAGEMENT is moving people and the organisation from the current state, through any interim states, to the target state, and is described in the change management approach (part of the people element). CHANGE CONTROL governs changes to the project's baseline: a request for change is a type of issue, handled under the issues practice and the issue management approach. Test: what is changing, the baseline (issues practice) or the organisation's way of working (people)? The communication management approach is separate again: it sets how information flows with stakeholders."},
    {"label": "Adopting vs Embedding vs Tailoring", "parentTopic": "Tailoring, Adopting & Embedding PRINCE2", objective: "5.4", "keyword": "ADOPTING is the organisation tailoring PRINCE2 once to produce its own PRINCE2-based method. EMBEDDING is the step after: making that method actually used through training, templates, assurance and habit. TAILORING happens at both levels; the project manager, with the board's approval, tailors the organisation's method again for a specific project and records the decisions in the project initiation documentation. Tailoring changes the depth, format and frequency of products and reports, the number of stages and the terminology. It never removes a principle, and the question each product answers must still be answerable."},
    {"label": "Directing, Controlling and Delivering: Who Runs Which Process", "parentTopic": "Directing a Project (DP)", objective: "2.2", "keyword": "DIRECTING A PROJECT is the project board's process and runs throughout; the board authorises initiation, each stage, exceptions and closure, and does not manage day to day. CONTROLLING A STAGE is the project manager's day-to-day process: authorising work packages, monitoring, handling issues and risks, reporting. MANAGING PRODUCT DELIVERY is the team manager's process of accepting a work package, producing the products and reporting back. MANAGING A STAGE BOUNDARY prepares the next stage plan and the end stage report. A mid-stage tolerance breach goes up from controlling a stage to directing a project through an exception report, not through the stage boundary process."}
  ],

